THE SAMPLING ARITHMETIC, which needs no GPU and settles the shape of
the answer before anything is run.

  one 54 us launch at     10 kHz ->   0.54 samples, NOT usable
  one 54 us launch at    100 kHz ->   5.40 samples, NOT usable
  one 54 us launch at    200 kHz ->  10.80 samples, NOT usable

So a single launch of the cell this study cares about cannot be measured
at any rate nsys offers. Back-to-back launches merged into one window:

      1.0 ms contiguous at 10 kHz ->     10.0 samples, edge 20.00%, alpha to +/-0.3116
     10.0 ms contiguous at 10 kHz ->    100.0 samples, edge  2.00%, alpha to +/-0.0312
    100.0 ms contiguous at 10 kHz ->   1000.0 samples, edge  0.20%, alpha to +/-0.0031
   1000.0 ms contiguous at 10 kHz ->  10000.0 samples, edge  0.02%, alpha to +/-0.0003

and the SAME 10 ms of kernel time profiled as separate windows rather than merged:

      1 window(s) over 10 ms total -> edge    2.00%, usable
     10 window(s) over 10 ms total -> edge   20.00%, NOT usable
    100 window(s) over 10 ms total -> edge  200.00%, NOT usable
   1000 window(s) over 10 ms total -> edge 2000.00%, NOT usable

The edge term is per WINDOW. Adding launches without merging them buys
samples and no accuracy, which is the single thing to get right in the
workload design and the reason every workload here goes idle before its
timed loop and never synchronises inside it.

